#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1036f694-7913-53be-8fe7-54060ff0f29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476229} {:answer/id #uuid "435a06fe-464c-5619-a04c-b90ad31d6ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476349} {:answer/id #uuid "967ed33f-f676-5b92-aad4-1d38a4658979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476530} {:answer/id #uuid "97a49745-03be-5585-991c-e769b83f8c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476534} {:answer/id #uuid "c81f6f7e-73b1-5545-9ab0-3dfc2ba67a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97a49745-03be-5585-991c-e769b83f8c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476534} :answer-field/id #uuid "4036f65d-4a9f-5e2e-aa37-47bc9e7f7c6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0e6acf-17b8-5853-9480-fac608a33b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-148230" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The outcome after tossing a fair coin.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $2$ and $5$ at random.\n\nIII. The letter obtained when a letter from the word Mississippi is chosen at random." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of possible outcomes after tossing a coin: Head and tail, and since the coin is fair, each outcome has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $2$ and $5,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because so letters occur more often than others. For example, there is a lower probability of selecting the letter \"M\".\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18be3bb8-ad9a-59a0-8e96-a2e78ebe90d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476246} {:answer/id #uuid "3e724cf9-e700-5717-b132-e0b59222dcbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476340} {:answer/id #uuid "556001da-9106-5edd-8a11-feee34eef773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476383} {:answer/id #uuid "95680a68-d4fb-5598-89ee-cb697902cdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476523} {:answer/id #uuid "b9aeb474-76b7-58b7-8e98-f5f416fc7fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9aeb474-76b7-58b7-8e98-f5f416fc7fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476628} :answer-field/id #uuid "db741232-8680-5d6a-bb33-fb3ba535c7c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7feb9bd7-7758-5cb1-b94d-d4d0a23f7141" :question/math-academy-id "q-148275" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of visitors to a museum in an hour.\n\nII. The number obtained when rolling a fair die.\n\nIII. The amount of time, in minutes, between successive full charges in a smartphone." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of visitors can be any positive integer. Moreover, each number of visitors is not equally likely.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers as possible outcomes, between $1$ and $6,$ and since the die is fair, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87432d69-96bb-52d4-838d-ce1f6c01f820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166233} {:answer/id #uuid "f17e4e3f-84c4-5252-b652-12737eea7c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166234} {:answer/id #uuid "ff361c42-9dab-56e8-a7fa-2e6229a27e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166235} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8d4f72-411d-5d3f-88dd-11650e216ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166236} {:answer/id #uuid "59e50954-5813-5a9b-9c99-a4c994779e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59e50954-5813-5a9b-9c99-a4c994779e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48a4e74-626d-5c6c-a256-eb8d5fc6d57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10558913-f96c-5656-8d0d-6962114123ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-97231" :question/problem "In a game show, a contestant can choose a prize from any box. The boxes are labeled with numbers $5$ through $15.$ If the contestant chooses a box at random, what is the probability that the box number they pick is less than $10?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,15\\}$ has $|S|=15-5+1=11$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11}, & x=5,6,…,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<10$ are $5,6,7,8,9.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9113dee1-1184-5d4f-aa27-da463ed39bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476514} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d64684-dede-539a-83c4-b43a77b362ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} {:answer/id #uuid "af717fad-e100-59bb-b086-82c6e11d1bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476594} {:answer/id #uuid "b762868b-9280-5c3f-8b90-87d5f4955e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476618} {:answer/id #uuid "ca885fbf-1c70-5e4e-a280-d699b127bab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d64684-dede-539a-83c4-b43a77b362ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68816de-9c55-5f5d-8b88-343571543dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aef0f15c-e4b9-546e-bc50-4ed011233821" :question/math-academy-id "q-97675" :question/problem "A particular traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Each day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August has an equal probability of being chosen. What is the probability that the festival will take place before the $18$th of August next year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the date of the festival. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<18).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and the set $S=\\{16,17,…,25\\}$ has $|S|=25-16+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=16,17,…,25 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<18$ are $16,17.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<18)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2002af14-f31e-5774-95b4-7d9d82ee7285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476275} {:answer/id #uuid "207fa16c-fd2e-53b6-a610-778aebbf45b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III ony" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476278} {:answer/id #uuid "3481be1a-af70-5da3-9fd8-e272a09d8a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476320} {:answer/id #uuid "71900d00-b9e3-581f-9e4c-75f17d668885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476459} {:answer/id #uuid "75fcae69-fdbf-5035-96b4-24a5fcae7c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71900d00-b9e3-581f-9e4c-75f17d668885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476459} :answer-field/id #uuid "23db8232-d6d6-5122-80a2-143130bc3231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb3352e8-034f-58da-933d-623fe3ee6c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-97756" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when spinning a roulette wheel that is biased towards landing on $0.$\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a rational number between $0$ and $1$ at random.\n\nIII. The IQ of a randomly selected student." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $0.$\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many rational numbers between $0$ and $1,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there are a small number of students with very low and very high IQs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}]}
